// INT8 Symmetric Quantization Kernel
//
// STATUS: both kernels typecheck. `as T` is implemented as a numeric cast
// (lexer token, postfix parse rule, and a checked inference rule that
// rejects non-numeric operands and targets).
//
// Quantities (QTT):
//   input   [1]     linear, consumed exactly once
//   output  inout   linear, written in place; needs an explicit quantity
//   scale   f32     linear. It was originally [0], which is right in spirit
//                   (read-only across the loop) but wrong in practice: a [0]
//                   variable is erased, and the typechecker correctly rejects
//                   it in a runtime position. Read-only-across-the-loop is not
//                   yet expressible.
//   N is a literal (1024) because the parser requires a literal in
//   Tensor[T, N] position; a symbolic extent is not expressible yet.
//
// A `proof { .. }` block now exists: it parses, is typechecked in its own
// scope, and reaches the AST for the prover to read. `assert` is proof-only --
// using it at runtime is a type error, so it can never be a silent no-op.
//
// LOWERING AND CODEGEN (as of this commit):
//   The lowering pass gives each `forall` a real iteration domain and schedule
//   band, and there is a WGSL COMPUTE backend:
//
//     naso build --target wgsl --kernel <name> -o out.wgsl <file>
//
//   An f32 or i32 tensor kernel emits a valid @compute entry point: tensors
//   become storage bindings, access mode comes from the quantity/mutability
//   annotations ([1] input -> read, inout output -> read_write), and one
//   invocation handles one element.
//
//   THIS FILE IS REFUSED, AND THAT IS CORRECT. It is a genuine i8 kernel, and
//   WGSL has no i8 storage type. Binding it as i32 would silently widen every
//   element and drop the `as i8` narrowing below, producing a shader that
//   VALIDATES and is still WRONG. So the backend refuses, naming the reason.
//
//   The width is now carried in the AST (Type::int_width) so that decision can
//   be made rather than guessed; previously i8 and i32 were indistinguishable
//   and the compiler widened silently. A narrower-than-i32 storage layout --
//   packed 4x i8 per u32, say -- is the real fix and is NOT implemented.
//
//   The extent is likewise now retained by the parser, so the entry point's
//   bounds guard is derived from the source rather than assumed.
//
//   So the ladder for this file: parses -> typechecks -> lowers to PIR with
//   correct iteration domains -> REFUSED at WGSL codegen with a stated reason.
//   An f32 variant of this kernel WOULD emit. The proof obligations are still
//   NOT discharged (float reasoning is unsupported and reported as unproved,
//   not as proved).

/// Quantize f32 -> i8 with a symmetric scale.
///
/// The cast can overflow unless the precondition holds, which is what a
/// proof should establish:
///
///   forall i in 0..1024 { abs(input[i] / scale) <= 127.0 }
///
/// This obligation is stated but not discharged (see the file header). The
/// clamp is therefore load-bearing rather than decorative: it is what keeps
/// the narrowing in range if the precondition is violated, and it is also
/// what makes the operation lossy.
fn quantize_int8_symmetric(
    input: [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[i8, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    proof {
        assert(scale > 0.0);
        assert(forall i in 0..1024 { abs(input[i] / scale) <= 127.0 });
    }

    forall i in 0..1024 {
        let v = round(input[i] / scale);
        output[i] = clamp(v, -128.0, 127.0) as i8;
    }
}

/// Dequantize i8 -> f32, widening each element and rescaling.
fn dequantize_int8_symmetric(
    input: [1] Tensor[i8, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    forall i in 0..1024 {
        let v = input[i] as f32;
        output[i] = v * scale;
    }
}

/// f32 -> f32 using the same prelude intrinsics a quantizer needs, with no
/// cast involved. Useful as a smoke test that forall loops, tensor indexing
/// and the math builtins all typecheck.
fn normalize_f32(
    input: [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    forall i in 0..1024 {
        output[i] = round(input[i] / scale);
    }
}

// BRANCHING AND LINEARITY
// ------------------------
// A `[1]` input must be consumed on EVERY path or NONE. This was previously
// unenforced: a consume inside one `if` arm or one `match` arm was silently
// accepted, so a quantum resource could leak on the paths that did not consume
// it. The typechecker now joins each branch's consumption state and rejects a
// value consumed on only some paths, so a circuit cannot conditionally discard a
// qubit without saying so.
//
// This kernel has no branches, so it does not exercise that check; see
// crates/naso-verify/tests/corpus_tests.rs (branch-join tests) and
// crates/naso-verify/tests/corpus/linearity/invalid.naso (leak cases).
